# Path-based git attributes
# https://www.kernel.org/pub/software/scm/git/docs/gitattributes.html

# @guards-index guard `* text=auto`
#
# whether a CRLF file is committed as CRLF depending on each contributor's `core.autocrlf`

# Line endings: LF in the repository, and whatever the platform wants in a working tree.
# Without this, whether a CRLF file is committed as CRLF depends on each contributor's
# core.autocrlf — `true` on the machine this package is written on, unset elsewhere. No
# `eol=` is set, so a Windows checkout still gets the CRLF that CHANGELOG.md and README.md
# are edited with, and every blob in this repository is already LF.
* text=auto

# Ignore all test and documentation with "export-ignore".
/.gitattributes     export-ignore
/.gitignore         export-ignore
/tests              export-ignore
/.editorconfig      export-ignore

# Runbooks for whoever cuts the release, not for whoever installs the package. The
# docs/ records are left in: they explain why the middleware behaves as it does, which
# is worth having beside the code.
#
# So nothing the package ships links to them: the README names both and the changelog says
# the numbering is recorded in `RELEASING.md`, and neither one links. A dead link is how a
# reader who has the package learns that a runbook exists and that they will never be handed
# it, which is a worse thing to leave in their path than a sentence that names a file.
/RELEASING.md       export-ignore
/PUSHING.md         export-ignore

# The lane a release is cut from, in one line. `bin/release.php` reads it as its default branch, the
# release-lane reading holds every other statement of it to this one, and the workflow reads it to
# check that the branch it gates is the branch a version comes from. It is this repository's own
# statement, so it is not installed beside the package either.
/.release-lane      export-ignore

# Development tooling: these drive this repository's own gate and releases, and reach for
# tools that are require-dev, so an installer has nothing to run them against.
/bin/bootstrap.php  export-ignore
/bin/checks.php     export-ignore
/bin/commit.php     export-ignore
/bin/release.php    export-ignore

# Two Windows conveniences rather than programs of the gate: a Composer run through a CA bundle
# rebuilt for a machine whose TLS is being intercepted, and a link that points a Laravel application
# at this checkout. Both are started by hand, on Windows, by whoever is working on this repository —
# an installer has neither the platform nor the checkout they assume, and neither script is reachable
# from anything under `src/`.
/bin/composer-ca.ps1   export-ignore
/bin/composer-link.cmd export-ignore

# The agentic layer: the rules an agent reads, the skills it loads, the checks that keep the two
# honest, and a pointer file for each of the ten tools that expect one. A consumer installs the
# middleware and the config; none of this is anything they run. It is left in the repository rather
# than hidden because it is the reasoning behind the code - and `.agents/machine.local.json`, which
# is the only file in it that names a machine, is gitignored rather than exported.
/AGENTS.md          export-ignore
/CLAUDE.md          export-ignore
/GEMINI.md          export-ignore
/.agents            export-ignore
/.skills            export-ignore
/.trae              export-ignore
/.cursor            export-ignore
/.continue          export-ignore
/.junie             export-ignore
/.kiro              export-ignore
/.windsurf          export-ignore

# The release inventory: what the last release shipped, written by bin/release.php into the
# release commit so the next release can weigh against it. Nothing in an installed package reads
# it, and a host app has no tag to describe.
/files.tsv          export-ignore
/methods.tsv        export-ignore

# One row per line, read back with an `explode` on the tab: a carriage return left on the last
# cell would make two identical rows compare unequal, in the weighing and in `--check` alike.
*.tsv               text eol=lf

# A batch file is the one shape here whose correctness turns on its line ending: cmd.exe reads it
# through `goto` and `call` and wants the ending a Windows filesystem hands it, while the general
# `text=auto` above leaves that to each contributor's `core.autocrlf`. This script jumps between
# labels from its first line to its last, so the ending is pinned rather than left to a local
# setting. `eol=` is a checkout fact and nothing else: the blob is stored LF like every other file
# in this repository.
*.cmd               text eol=crlf
